# Independent password lock

This unreleased version requires a separate app password even when Home Assistant is already signed in. All archive web content, controls, models and downloads are gated server-side. The legacy machine transcript bearer API is disabled. Native Home Assistant MQTT recording/transcription controls remain independent and unchanged; this is not a lock against administrators of Home Assistant or the host.

## Secure route prerequisite

An administrator must verify HTTPS access and the complete ingress route before filling app_https_hostname and app_ingress_core_peer. Both default empty and access stays locked. The implemented route is Tailscale Serve HTTPS to HA through 127.0.0.1, then HA Core and Supervisor ingress. It requires a pinned .ts.net hostname, exact selected forwarding headers, actual ingress socket peer 172.30.32.2 and the verified three-address chain [tailnet client,127.0.0.1,Core peer]. Do not infer the Core peer from generic examples or accept untrusted forwarded headers alone. Trusted host-network processes remain inside this network trust boundary; this is not cryptographic TLS attestation. Other HTTPS architectures require a separately reviewed configuration/design.

Tailscale-encrypted HTTP is still HTTP to the browser. It cannot satisfy this app's Secure cookie or pinned HTTPS gate. Do not bypass certificate warnings or enable public Funnel as a workaround.

## First-time setup on a phone

1. Connect Tailscale and open HA using its verified HTTPS address. After signing in, open this app and select Set up on this phone.
2. Generate a random setup code. Save it privately in a password manager before leaving the page. It is not stored in browser storage, cookies, logs or URLs. Do not send it in chat.
3. Copy only the separate approval hash:expiry value into this app's administrator-only Configuration field app_enrollment_approval. Leave app_password_verifier blank. Save and restart this app during an appropriate maintenance window.
4. Return to setup, enter the saved code and a new unique password twice. The fixed server-derived approval expires after 30 minutes and restarting does not extend it. Existing passwords cannot be overwritten through this page; first visitors cannot claim an unapproved app. The hash alone cannot enroll.
5. After success, clear the approval field and use Back to unlock. No session is issued automatically. Do not put the password or setup code in an HA option.

Use 12–256 characters; browser limits may count emoji differently from server Unicode codepoints. Lost or expired setup codes require a fresh pair and new administrator approval. If the connection is interrupted during submission, try unlocking first: creation may have completed.

## Storage and recovery

The server stores only a random-salt scrypt verifier (N32768,r8,p1) in /data/app-password.json, created exclusively with mode 0600 and synced to disk. This private file and backups permit offline guessing and must not be published. A present file takes precedence over the legacy app_password_verifier option. Corrupt, unreadable, symlinked or nonregular file state fails closed and never falls back to the old option. If no file exists, any nonempty legacy option blocks first-time web enrollment.

The private interactive password_admin.py tool is a legacy alternative before file enrollment, not a way to replace a file-enrolled password. There is no unauthenticated reset, delete or overwrite endpoint. Recovery requires a separately authorized administrator procedure: stop the app, preserve a private copy of verifier/options, establish the failure state, deliberately move the old verifier aside and clear the legacy verifier, then approve a fresh setup. Do not delete archive data or grant broad host/API access for recovery by default. Restoring a verifier backup restores its old password.

## Sessions and limits

Sessions use random 256-bit tokens in Secure,HttpOnly,SameSite=Strict cookies scoped and bound to the HA ingress path. They expire after 15 minutes idle or 8 hours total; status polling does not extend idle time. Restart clears sessions. Login attempts have a shared five-per-five-minute budget, and at most 16 sessions are held. Logout revokes the session and stops later application stream writes; bytes already sent and downloaded files cannot be revoked. No secrets are stored in browser local/session storage.

Before production acceptance, validate the actual HTTPS proxy route, phone setup, Secure cookies, wrong-password limits, logout, expiration, back navigation and interrupted downloads. Synthetic test results do not prove a live deployment is secure. Preserve existing data/options and validate a bounded rollback before updating an existing installation.
